³1ip³265.219.208.102³2ts³21097751196³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3= Amateur Radio Packet Switching =

&nbsp;

Excerpted from: http://www.instantweb.com/foldoc/foldoc.cgi?packet+radio

Packet Radio. A complete amateur radio computer network with "digipeaters" (relays), mailboxes (BBS) and other special nodes. Satellites with OSCAR (Orbiting Sattelite Carring Amateur Radio) transponders carry Packet Radio mailboxes or digipeaters. PR uses AX.25 as its transport layer and sometimes even TCP/IP. There are special "wormholes" all over the world which "tunnel" amateur radio traffic through the Internet to forward mail. Because amateur radio is for non profit, it must not be interconnected to the Internet but it may be connected through the Internet. All people must be licensed radio amateurs having a "call" which is unique all over the world. There is a special domain AMPR.ORG for amateur radio reserved in the IP space. Mail transport is only one aspect of packet radio. You can talk interactively, read files, or play games. Paths over 1000 km are unlikely to be useable for real-time communication. Usenet newsgroup: rec.radio.amateur.packet. 

- - -

